      "<p>Medical office replacement sourcing identifies healthcare real estate for Denver, Colorado investors completing a Section 1031 exchange into a sector known for long lease terms, provider credit backing, and comparatively resilient demand across economic cycles. Medical office buildings, whether leased to hospital systems, physician groups, or ambulatory surgery operators, often carry higher tenant improvement costs and more specialized building systems than standard office space, which changes both the underwriting approach and the pool of qualified buyers and lenders active in the sector.</p><p>We source candidates against the investor's target criteria, including building size, tenant mix, and proximity to hospital campuses or population density that supports patient volume, and we prioritize properties with leases backed by health systems or established physician groups over single practitioner tenants where lease renewal risk is comparatively higher. Each candidate package includes lease abstract summaries, tenant credit background, and a comparable analysis against recent medical office transactions in the target market.</p><h3>Provider credit and building specialization</h3><p>Medical office underwriting requires attention to tenant credit that goes beyond a standard corporate guarantee review. A lease to a large regional health system carries a materially different risk profile than a lease to an independent physician group, even when both show similar current revenue, because health system leases are frequently supported by a broader balance sheet and strategic real estate planning rather than the economics of a single location. We evaluate the tenant's credit background, remaining lease term, and any system affiliation or network relationship that supports the durability of the income stream.</p><p>Building specialization also affects replacement cost and re-tenanting risk. Medical office space often includes plumbing, electrical, and structural improvements built for a specific clinical use, which can limit the pool of replacement tenants if the current tenant vacates. We flag buildings with highly specialized build outs so the investor understands re-tenanting exposure separately from the strength of the current lease.</p><h3>Meeting exchange deadlines with a specialized asset pool</h3><p>Because qualified medical office inventory is more limited than standard multifamily or retail product, sourcing typically begins well before the relinquished property closes so the investor has vetted candidates in hand when the forty five day identification window opens. We track available inventory across target markets continuously rather than starting a search from scratch once a sale is under contract, since medical office transactions can take longer to reach a signed purchase agreement than more liquid asset classes.</p><p>Identification follows the standard IRS framework. Under the three property rule, up to three properties may be identified regardless of value, which is often sufficient when a single strong medical office candidate has been located along with reasonable backups. Where the investor is considering several smaller medical office assets, the two hundred percent rule may apply instead, and we confirm the applicable threshold before finalizing the identification letter delivered to the qualified intermediary. A Section 1031 exchange defers capital gains and depreciation recapture tax rather than eliminating it, and boot is created whenever cash is withdrawn or replacement debt falls below relinquished debt without an offsetting cash contribution. This service provides sourcing and coordination support and is not legal, tax, or investment advice; Denver, Colorado investors should confirm structure with their qualified intermediary and CPA.</p><p>Denver's growing population and expanding healthcare footprint support demand for medical office space both within Colorado and in comparable growth markets nationally, and because Colorado taxes capital gains at a flat rate alongside ordinary income, Denver, Colorado investors exiting appreciated property have a strong incentive to complete a full value replacement rather than accept a partial exchange. We track medical office inventory across several growth markets in parallel so a Denver investor is not limited to whatever is available locally within the compressed identification window.</p><p>We also evaluate parking ratio, ADA accessibility, and any certificate of need considerations that can affect a medical office building's marketability to future tenants, since these factors are more specific to healthcare real estate than to standard office product. A building that scores well on lease term and tenant credit but falls short on parking or accessibility for its market can face harder re-tenanting if the current tenant does not renew, and we flag this during sourcing so the investor is weighing the full picture rather than lease terms alone.</p>",
